---

HDR needs Python 3.11 or later and has no runtime dependencies.

# Install the CLI

```sh
uv tool install .
hdr --help
```

Or run it from a source checkout without installing anything:

```sh
python3 -m hdr --help
```

# Seed the decision log

From the root of the repository you want to govern:

```sh
hdr init --harness claude
```

Pass `--harness` once for each harness your team uses (`claude`, `codex`,
`opencode`, `pi`). It defaults to `claude`. `init` creates two decisions, each
with a one-entry history:

| Decision | What it says |
| --- | --- |
| `HDR-001` | Decision histories are append-only, and only people change existing decisions. |
| `HDR-002` | The HDR checker is registered for every lifecycle event in each listed harness. |

It also writes `.hdr/.gitignore` so run reports in `.hdr/runs/` stay out of
version control. `init` refuses to run if `.hdr` already exists.

# Register the checker

Install the hook for each harness, either for this repository only or for your
user account:

```sh
hdr install claude --project
hdr install pi --user
```

A project install is committed with the repository. A user install covers every
repository on your machine; repositories without a `.hdr` directory are
ignored. Each install prints what to do next, which is usually to restart the
harness. See [Harnesses]($link.page('harnesses')) for the details of each one.

># [Note]($block)
>The installed command contains the absolute path of your Python interpreter and
>of the HDR source. Install on each machine, and reinstall after moving the
>checkout or replacing the interpreter.

# Check the wiring

```sh
hdr check
```

`check` validates every record, confirms the checker is registered where
`HDR-002` says it should be, evaluates state checks, and saves a run report. Exit
code 0 means everything passed, 1 means a check failed, and 2 means invalid input
or an error.

To see the whole picture in a browser:

```sh
hdr explain
```

This writes `.hdr/runs/report.html` and opens it. It shows the decision log
(with each decision's history), hook coverage per harness and event, configuration
findings, and the most recent runs.

# Add your own decisions

Write a new file in `.hdr/decisions/` with the next free ID and one history
entry, then run `hdr validate`. It prints how many decisions are current. Read
[Writing decisions]($link.page('decisions')) first: histories are append-only, so a
decision you add stays in the repository for good, even once it is retired.
